WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.136
Before anything else, picture this.

00:00:02.536 --> 00:00:09.943
Power fails halfway through a money transfer. After reboot, did the money vanish? Transactions and the write-ahead log say no.

00:00:11.143 --> 00:00:12.072
Pause and think.

00:00:12.472 --> 00:00:15.781
How does MVCC let readers avoid blocking writers?

00:00:16.181 --> 00:00:23.495
Hold your answer; we will check it at the end. And whenever a new word comes up, we stop for a moment and explain it.

00:00:24.695 --> 00:00:30.674
A transaction groups changes so they all happen or none happen, even if the machine crashes halfway.

00:00:32.024 --> 00:00:34.869
Think of moving money between two jars at home.

00:00:35.319 --> 00:00:39.324
You first write in a notebook: move ten dollars from jar A to jar B.

00:00:39.774 --> 00:00:45.823
Then you move the money. If you are interrupted, the notebook tells you exactly what to finish or undo.

00:00:46.273 --> 00:00:50.220
That notebook is the write ahead log, written before the real change.

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:53.672
A C I D in plain words.

00:00:53.695 --> 00:01:07.801
New word: A C I D. Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability: guarantees of reliable database transactions. Picture it like this: A bank transfer that either fully happens or doesn't at all.

00:01:09.445 --> 00:01:11.140
Atomic: all or nothing.

00:01:11.162 --> 00:01:22.784
New word: atomic. An operation that happens all at once; no other thread can see it half-done. Picture it like this: Flipping a light switch — it's either on or off, never half.

00:01:24.412 --> 00:01:28.487
Consistent: rules like balances never going negative still hold.

00:01:28.937 --> 00:01:33.581
Isolated: concurrent transactions do not see each other's half finished work.

00:01:34.031 --> 00:01:39.940
Durable: once committed, it survives a crash, because the log was flushed to disk first.

00:01:41.290 --> 00:01:45.563
Multi version concurrency control keeps several versions of each row.

00:01:46.013 --> 00:01:50.285
A writer adds a new version with its transaction time instead of overwriting.

00:01:50.735 --> 00:01:54.834
A reader that started at time five keeps seeing the version from time three.

00:01:55.284 --> 00:01:59.417
So readers never block writers, and old versions are cleaned up later.

00:02:00.767 --> 00:02:04.377
How nested transactions work in the practical interview store.

00:02:04.827 --> 00:02:06.743
Begin pushes an undo log.

00:02:07.193 --> 00:02:10.653
Each set records the old value in the top undo log.

00:02:11.103 --> 00:02:17.256
Rollback replays it in reverse; commit merges it into the parent, or discards it at the top level.

00:02:17.378 --> 00:02:24.692
New word: rollback. Going back to the previous working version. Picture it like this: Undoing to the last saved game.

00:02:27.228 --> 00:02:34.461
Log first, then change. Keep versions so readers see a stable snapshot. Undo logs make rollback simple.

00:02:34.435 --> 00:02:35.689
Back to the question.

00:02:36.089 --> 00:02:39.398
How does MVCC let readers avoid blocking writers?

00:02:39.798 --> 00:02:43.908
Readers see an older committed version while writers create a new one.

00:02:45.108 --> 00:02:45.978
Quick recap.

00:02:46.378 --> 00:02:47.447
All or nothing.

00:02:47.847 --> 00:02:49.379
Log before you change.

00:02:49.779 --> 00:02:52.206
Versions let reads and writes overlap.
